WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.000
The natural world is full of extraordinary animals with amazing life histories.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:12.000
Yet certain stories are more intriguing than most.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:18.000
The mysteries of a butterfly's life cycle.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:22.000
Or the strange biology of the Ampropenguin.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:29.000
Some of these creatures were surrounded by myths and misunderstandings for a very long time.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:33.000
And some have only recently revealed their secrets.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:37.000
These are the animals that stand out from the crowd.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:42.000
The curiosities I find most fascinating of all.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:54.000
Animals are usually either male or female.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:59.000
And usually they behave in a way that is characteristic of their gender.

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:04.000
But in nature there are always curious exceptions.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:10.000
Female hyenas behave and look like males.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:16.000
And male seahorses play mother and physically give birth.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:24.000
Only now are we beginning to understand why these two animals seem to have swapped their sexual identities.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.000
Seahorses are fascinating.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:37.000
Some are tiny and blend perfectly with their surroundings.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:42.000
Others could grow to an impressive 35 centimeters in size.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:47.000
They live in shallow waters, both tropical and temperate, across much of the world.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:51.000
And have even been found in the Tempestery near London.

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:55.000
Seeing one for the first time is a moment to remember.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:02:00.000
They're magical creatures with a truly fantastic appearance.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:11.000
They have the head of a horse, eyes like a chameleon, the prehensile tail of a monkey, armor that can change color, and perhaps most strangely of all, a pouch.

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:22.000
Their unusual features inspired their name, hippocampus, a combination of two Greek words, hippo meaning horse and campos meaning sea monster.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:26.000
For centuries they've been considered animals of myth and legend.

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:33.000
And only today are we unraveling the true story of males that give birth.

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:38.000
Seahorses baffled early naturalists.

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:42.000
Their unusual characteristics seemed to make them misfits.

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:47.000
But after much debate, they were recognized as true bony fish.

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:51.000
But their breeding habits were hardly fishy.

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:59.000
Typically, female fish release large numbers of eggs into the sea that males must quickly fertilize.

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:05.000
But a fish that kept its eggs in a pouch seems scarcely believable.

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:10.000
The seahorse's striking appearance has given it an almost magical status.

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:17.000
Images and stories of a creature, part horse, part fish, have spanned the centuries across many cultures.

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:21.000
Among the most famous are those belonging to Poseidon.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:30.000
This famous Greek god of the sea lived below the waves and his golden chariot was pulled by a pair of giant hippocampi.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:36.000
The seahorse's odd behavior appeared mysterious too. As early as the third century BC,

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:43.000
Aristotle noted in his book on the history of animals that pipefish, close relatives of the seahorse,

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:49.000
had a pouch that burst into two to release the young.

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:53.000
These early observations of the pipefish's strange breeding behavior

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:59.000
helped to reveal the true story of the male seahorse's mysterious pouch.

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:04.000
Just like seahorses, pipefish carry their eggs around with them.

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:09.000
Some species simply stick the eggs to the outside of their bodies.

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:13.000
Others have a rudimentary pouch.

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:25.000
These simpler techniques provide some clues as to how seahorses developed their more complex, closed pouch.

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:32.000
But what Aristotle didn't know when he spotted the pipefish giving birth was that he was actually looking at a male.

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:42.000
And this important detail was to remain undiscovered for hundreds of years.

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:50.000
Although seahorses live in British waters, until Victorian times few people apart from fishermen had ever seen them.

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:59.000
In 1859, Mr Pinto brought four live seahorses back to London from the mouth of the river Tagus in Portugal.

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:09.000
Pinto endured a sleepless seven-day train journey through Europe, waking himself frequently to aerate the seahorse's water with a syringe.

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:18.000
His seahorses survived and were installed in the new London aquarium. They were an instant hit.

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:25.000
Seahorses were headline news. Mr Pinto's journey and their arrival made the front pages.

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:32.000
Now they could be seen in great detail and the study of their mysterious breeding began.

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:40.000
In that same year, what was described as a herd of baby seahorses was born in the British Midlands aquarium.

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:49.000
This caused quite a stir, as did the discovery that it was the male that gave birth to the young.

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:58.000
But why seahorses swap parenting roles remained a mystery and we're still searching for the answers today.

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:04.000
Here at the London Zoo's aquarium, over 150 years since the arrival of the first seahorses,

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:10.000
a detailed study is revealing more about their reproduction and the unusual role of the male.

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:21.000
These tanks are set like a seahorse dating centre. The first port of call is the courtship aquarium, or ballroom tank.

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:27.000
Here, a number of adult seahorses spend time getting to know each other as they look for compatible partners.

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:38.000
Reigning seahorses form lasting partnerships as mating pairs and their long, elaborate courtship dances are a way of finding and securing a suitable mate.

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:49.000
Dances, like those of this Australian species, can be complex and last several days.

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:57.000
They help the couple synchronise their bodies so that the male's pouch is ready for the eggs.

00:06:57.000 --> 00:07:05.000
They also help to establish the couple's joint territory.

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:14.000
Seahorses were thought to be monogamous, but we now know that some are only exclusive couples for the duration of the breeding season.

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:22.000
The female must choose the right male because she's going to pass over her precious eggs to him.

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:31.000
Female seahorses do not have a pouch, so a strong pair bond with the male is very important as he will care for her eggs.

00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:43.000
This is the honeymoon tank. Seahorses that have shown an attraction for each other in the courtship tank are removed as a couple and given their own private space.

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:49.000
In the wild, each pair has its own territory and these smaller tanks make captive breeding more successful.

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:55.000
Here, the pair can synchronise their courtship. Timing is crucial.

00:07:55.000 --> 00:08:03.000
The female's eggs must be fully developed at exactly the same time that the male's pouch is ready to receive them.

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:13.000
Once the female's eggs are ready, she hydrates them with seawater. They must then be laid within 24 hours.

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:23.000
She transfers her eggs to her partner by inserting her egg laying tube or ovipositor into the male's pouch.

00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:31.000
Once pregnant, the male attaches himself to one spot and the female visits him every day.

00:08:31.000 --> 00:08:38.000
She checks to see when he'll be ready for her next batch of eggs.

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:50.000
One theory suggests that because the male is incubating the eggs, the female has more time to feed and can put energy into making new eggs more quickly.

00:08:50.000 --> 00:09:01.000
Swapping roles may be a smart way to use their resources more efficiently.

00:09:01.000 --> 00:09:06.000
What goes on inside the pouch is still a mystery.

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:18.000
The male may simply provide a closed incubator or the inner skin may develop extra blood vessels to give a more placenta-like connection. It's not clear.

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:33.000
During pregnancy and birth, the male's metabolism increases, but that's a little wonder, for he may have up to 1,500 eggs in his pouch.

00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:40.000
The male seahorse gives birth to dozens of miniature babies, perfect in every detail.

00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:46.000
The free-swimming young are put into separate crash tanks where they can be fed and cared for.

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:54.000
The parent seahorses in this biological hotel remain in the honeymoon suite, ready to mate again.

00:09:54.000 --> 00:10:06.000
These are some of last year's youngsters and they've grown enormously. Next year, they'll be breeding themselves.

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:12.000
Swapping the parental roles seems to work well for seahorses.

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:22.000
In warm conditions, a male can give birth every 28 to 30 days, but of the thousands of fry produced each year, only a few survive.

00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:27.000
There is no safe crash in the open sea.

00:10:27.000 --> 00:10:43.000
To succeed, seahorse parents must work well together. Yet in this partnership, the female seems to have the freedom to swim, feed and patrol a territory, which is normally the prerogative of the male.

00:10:43.000 --> 00:10:57.000
So, is the male seahorse a slave to a galavanting female? Well, latest research suggests not and shows that some males may have more control over breeding than first thought.

00:10:57.000 --> 00:11:05.000
If small or poor quality eggs are deposited into their pouches, some males will absorb them.

00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:16.000
Such males appear to be choosy about how they invest their time and energy. And some females, in entrusting their eggs to males, are being cheated.

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:27.000
But the male seahorse can't be duped, as having a pouch means that he can always be certain that all the baby seahorses he gives birth to are his own.

00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:34.000
For seahorses, having a male mother allows them to make the most of the breeding season.

00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:43.000
Next, another story of strange parenting. The female hyena, who looks and behaves just like a male.

00:11:43.000 --> 00:11:51.000
Male and female seahorses have swapped their roles. The male is the mother and he gives birth to the babies.

00:11:51.000 --> 00:12:08.000
In the case of the spotted hyena, it's the female that has changed, looking and behaving more like a male. Why have they become so masculine?

00:12:08.000 --> 00:12:17.000
These are African spotted hyenas, creatures that have an undeservedly bad reputation and a very strange biology.

00:12:17.000 --> 00:12:23.000
In the wild, they live in clans of up to 80 individuals and the females dominate the males.

00:12:23.000 --> 00:12:31.000
The females are big, aggressive and look physically almost exactly like males.

00:12:31.000 --> 00:12:40.000
Unraveling why the female is like this has not been easy, as it's difficult to tell the difference between the sexes.

00:12:40.000 --> 00:12:51.000
The female's male appearance is made all the more convincing by her reproductive organs. They're external and very similar to a male's.

00:12:51.000 --> 00:13:02.000
Understanding hyena biology has helped to explain the females' masculinity and the species' reputation as aggressive scavengers.

00:13:02.000 --> 00:13:09.000
But in the past, these strange traits gave hyenas a very bad image.

00:13:09.000 --> 00:13:17.000
In the first century, Pliny the Elder described the hyenas and did them a great disservice. This is what he wrote.

00:13:17.000 --> 00:13:28.000
Hyenas are like a cross between a dog and a wolf. They break everything with their teeth, swallow it as a gulp and masticate it in the belly.

00:13:28.000 --> 00:13:42.000
They are believed to become male and female in alternate years. They can imitate the human voice, calling a shepherd by name so that he comes outside where they tear him to pieces.

00:13:42.000 --> 00:13:49.000
Any animal that a hyena looks at three times will be unable to move.

00:13:49.000 --> 00:13:59.000
That tainted image of hyenas was perpetuated for many years to come and they were branded as evil, dangerous creatures.

00:13:59.000 --> 00:14:08.000
Hyenas are not, of course, evil, but their competitive nature and unusual eating habits make them appear fearsome.

00:14:08.000 --> 00:14:17.000
They're specialist feeders. They crush, eat and digest bones that other creatures can't tackle and so leave behind.

00:14:17.000 --> 00:14:28.000
And this diet has a significant effect on the female's appearance and her family relationships, especially those with her cubs.

00:14:28.000 --> 00:14:38.000
In the early 19th century, an unusual discovery in Britain excited one man to look more closely at the hyena's diet.

00:14:38.000 --> 00:14:50.000
In 1822, a rather eccentric but very eminent geologist called William Buckland made a significant discovery that was to further the modern understanding of hyenas.

00:14:50.000 --> 00:14:58.000
Quarrying workers in Kirkdale, Yorkshire, had come across a cave that contained a large number of bones.

00:14:58.000 --> 00:15:10.000
Buckland was very excited and rushed to see the remains before they were disturbed any further and he found that mud deposits in the cave had preserved the bones of over 22 different species of animals,

00:15:10.000 --> 00:15:23.000
including tiger, bear, wolf, elephant and significantly hyenas, which Buckland described as littering the cave like the bones in a dog kennel.

00:15:23.000 --> 00:15:32.000
This is one of the actual hyena jaws that Buckland found. It belonged to a young but ancient hyena.

00:15:32.000 --> 00:15:42.000
There are also a lot of these on the cave floor. They are coprolites or fossilised faeces from hyenas.

00:15:42.000 --> 00:15:53.000
They contain bone fragments that have passed through the hyena's digestive tract and so showed that they were successful bone crushers.

00:15:53.000 --> 00:16:02.000
Buckland's discovery of so many bones in what he believed to be a hyena's den indicated that they were very successful hunters.

00:16:02.000 --> 00:16:11.000
Contrary to popular belief, they scavenge very little and kill over 80% of their own food.

00:16:11.000 --> 00:16:21.000
A lone hyena can easily kill a wildebeest or a topi and with teamwork they will tackle bigger animals like zebra and giraffe.

00:16:21.000 --> 00:16:29.000
They do scavenge as well, but it's more usual for lions to steal from hyenas rather than the other way around.

00:16:29.000 --> 00:16:41.000
Female hyenas have become big and strong and compete for food with other members of their clan. Nothing goes to waste. They can eat even the thickest of bones.

00:16:41.000 --> 00:16:49.000
Buckland was fascinated by the marks on the bones from the cave, but found it hard to believe that hyenas had made them.

00:16:49.000 --> 00:16:58.000
He wanted to be sure of his findings and understand how their jaws with their strange massive teeth actually worked.

00:16:58.000 --> 00:17:09.000
Hyenas are African or Asiatic animals, so Buckland's discovery of hyena bones in an English cave was strange, to put it mildly.

00:17:09.000 --> 00:17:20.000
As a man of science, he wanted to confirm that the sculley he had collected from Corkdale was definitely from a hyena and that it had made the marks on the many fractured bones.

00:17:20.000 --> 00:17:29.000
To try and prove his case, he asked a friend, William Burchill, an African traveller, to send a young hyena back to England from the Cape.

00:17:29.000 --> 00:17:34.000
He planned to kill it and compare its skull and teeth with the specimens in the cave.

00:17:34.000 --> 00:17:43.000
The young hyena that arrived at the docks was already tame and had become a great favourite with the sailors who christened him Billy.

00:17:43.000 --> 00:17:52.000
Billy became quite a celebrity and was as tame as a pet dog. No-one could bring themselves to sacrifice him for the sake of science.

00:17:52.000 --> 00:18:00.000
Instead, a search of British museums produced a hyena's skull and Billy's life was spared.

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:05.000
Buckland was then able to compare the new and old skulls and they matched.

00:18:05.000 --> 00:18:11.000
Billy also helped to clarify the fractures on the bones. He was fed ox bones.

00:18:11.000 --> 00:18:20.000
This was one and Buckland compared it with one that was found in the cave and they closely match.

00:18:20.000 --> 00:18:28.000
This ability to crack massive bones explains why female hyenas look like bristling males.

00:18:28.000 --> 00:18:33.000
It's also tied up intricately with the relationship they have with their cubs.

00:18:33.000 --> 00:18:41.000
Cubs are born underground and are fed on their mother's rich milk.

00:18:41.000 --> 00:18:48.000
At about three months of age, they emerge from the den and continue to suckle for almost another two years.

00:18:48.000 --> 00:18:54.000
Their mother helps feed the youngsters as they can't yet crack and crush bones for themselves.

00:18:54.000 --> 00:19:04.000
Even at almost a year in age, when they're big enough to join the kill, their teeth and jaws are still not sufficiently developed to tackle big bones.

00:19:04.000 --> 00:19:09.000
The skull of a young hyena is quite different from that of an adult.

00:19:09.000 --> 00:19:15.000
It's got a flat top, narrow cheeks and relatively small teeth.

00:19:15.000 --> 00:19:21.000
An animal with a skull like this would not be able to crush and eat big bones.

00:19:21.000 --> 00:19:27.000
It takes almost three years for a young hyena's skull to grow to full size and reach mechanical maturity.

00:19:27.000 --> 00:19:29.000
And this is a result.

00:19:29.000 --> 00:19:36.000
This skull has a large, vaulted forehead that dissipates biting stress, carrying it away from the face.

00:19:36.000 --> 00:19:47.000
It's also got wide arches at its sides for the attachment of powerful jaw muscles and robust premolars that have specialized crack-resistant enamel.

00:19:47.000 --> 00:19:55.000
A skull just like these can crack the dense bones of zebra and even giraffe.

00:19:55.000 --> 00:20:00.000
Developing this substantial specialized eating equipment takes time.

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:05.000
So it may be several years before a young hyena can feed independently.

00:20:05.000 --> 00:20:11.000
This puts pressure on their mothers to become dominant and aggressive.

00:20:11.000 --> 00:20:15.000
They need to fight to get enough food for their cubs.

00:20:15.000 --> 00:20:21.000
This status in the clan's hierarchy will directly affect the survival of a young.

00:20:21.000 --> 00:20:27.000
The biggest, oldest, most established females are the most dominant and take a bigger share of the kill.

00:20:27.000 --> 00:20:34.000
So food and the need to fight for it has made females look and behave like aggressive males.

00:20:34.000 --> 00:20:37.000
But it has also had a strange side effect.

00:20:37.000 --> 00:20:46.000
Female hyenas have large amounts of the male hormone testosterone and consequently develop male-like reproductive organs.

00:20:46.000 --> 00:20:48.000
This can be a problem.

00:20:48.000 --> 00:20:57.000
Having a long, thin birth canal makes mating very difficult and both mothers and cubs sometimes die during birth.

00:20:57.000 --> 00:21:03.000
The female's strange gender swap is one of the most unusual in the animal kingdom.

00:21:03.000 --> 00:21:09.000
New science has now made sense of the old clues and solved this mystery.

00:21:09.000 --> 00:21:12.000
Hyenas are very intriguing animals.

00:21:12.000 --> 00:21:23.000
William Butland's early observations of their bones and his hyena experiments started a study of these creatures that was to reveal their fascinating biology.

00:21:23.000 --> 00:21:29.000
Hyenas may have a frightening reputation, but their odd characteristics all have a reason.

00:21:29.000 --> 00:21:37.000
The story of their aggression and bizarre bodies is intimately tied up with their food and the survival of their cubs.

00:21:37.000 --> 00:21:48.000
They've evolved a perfectly formed bone-breaking jaw, but the time it takes to grow has resulted in one of the most unusual but dedicated mothers in the animal kingdom.

00:21:48.000 --> 00:21:59.000
So to become the best parents, female hyenas have become more male and male seahorses more motherly.

00:22:18.000 --> 00:22:20.000
you
